{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And Jacob was left alone, and a man wrestled with him until the breaking of dawn.</b> Regarding the matter of the wrestling with Jacob, opinions are many — both short and long, a single tent too narrow to raise upon it all the opinions of the commentators. I said to myself: solutions belong to Elohim — I too will answer my portion and say: After most of the commentators agreed that this angel is Samael, the prince of Esav, who is called 'Samael-Esav' — for his entire desire and will is to blind a person's eyes and to strike him with blindness and to make him blind of the eye of the intellect, until the one who sees can no longer see visions of truth, come and see the face of HaShem, and enter the council of HaShem for those who fear Him in the secrets of the Torah — for he is Satan, he is the angel of death, he is the evil inclination (Bava Batra 16a). And a hint to the matter: Samael is the partner of עֲנָבִ״ים (grapes) in אַלְפָ״א בֵּיתָ״א of the secondary letters, for just as the wine that comes forth from grapes, when it intoxicates a person, behold he is blinded by the closing of his eyes, as it is said (Genesis 49:12) ״חַכְלִילִי עֵינַיִם מִיָּיִן״ ('dark of eyes from wine'), and any drunkard walks about as a blind man — so too this Samael is his partner: all his schemes are to blind a person's eyes until he cannot see the sun. And if so, grapes blind the physical eye, and this Samael, their partner, blinds the eye of the intellect, so that one does not see in the visions of El — and this is why סַמָּאֵל is a compound word [סַם + אֵל: the poison/blindness of El]." "And another thing you must know about him: the evil inclination is likened to a fly (Berakhot 61a), as I explained above in the portion of Bereshit on the verse ״לַפֶּתַח חַטָּאת רוֹבֵץ״ (Genesis 4:7) — for an ordinary fly does not alight upon whole flesh on a person, for its mouth has no power to make a new opening in whole flesh. Therefore the fly alights between the lips, in a place where it finds an opening slightly ajar; there it settles, to widen the opening of the wound. So too the evil inclination does not confront a whole person who has not broken his fence at all and has not opened for himself an opening to sin and to impurity — and with a person who is whole in all his deeds, it has no relationship or business whatsoever, for it has no power over him at all. But at the time when a person himself opens for it a slight opening and breaks his fence even through a minor transgression, then immediately the evil inclination confronts him and says to him, 'You are none but my own bone and flesh,' and then it will ascend from minor transgressions to grave ones, and will strive to widen the breach until the violence of man rises without remedy, and it has broken his fence and made it a trampling-ground (Isaiah 5:5). So too this angel Samael, the prince of Esav — when he saw that the affairs of Jacob with Esav depend on the observance of the Torah, as it is written ״וְהָיָה כַּאֲשֶׁר תָּרִיד״ etc. (Genesis 27:40) — behold, as long as he beheld no iniquity in Jacob at all, he did not confront him to blind the eye of his intellect completely. But when he saw that Jacob had broken his fence slightly in this departure — for Elohim had shown him kindness and given him everything, an abundance of wealth and possessions and honor, and Jacob had departed somewhat from the measure of contentment — ״וַיִּוָּתֵר יַעֲקֹב לְבַדּוֹ״, and some say ״לְכַדּוֹ״ ('to trap him'), in a place of danger, for the night is the gathering-time for all the harmful spirits, and he remained alone in the night on account of small jars, high and exalted cables, and for the sake of a trifling matter held in ropes of contempt, he remained alone in a place of danger. Then Samael said, 'Aha! I have found iniquity for me' — since Jacob had begun with a flaw and had made himself somewhat blind of his own eyes, for who is blind like the lovers of money, about whom it is said (Proverbs 27:20) ״וְעֵינֵי הָאָדָם לֹא תִשְׂבַּעְנָה״? Then Samael said, 'Behold, the time has come to draw near and to embrace him, and I will add to him impurity to fill his eyes with dust and dirt, so that he will be altogether blind — even of the eye of the intellect.' And this is what it says: ״וַיִּוָּתֵר יַעֲקֹב לְבַדּוֹ״ — when he passed beyond the boundary of contentment, וַיִּוָּתֵר 'alone, to trap him,' and he is alone, for his mind had departed from all mankind: for what simpleton would turn aside to endanger himself for such a trifling matter? It is nothing but a suspended heart that has bent him from the path of the upright, to love money so much — which causes forgetting of Eloah from above, as it is written (Deuteronomy 8:13–14) ״וְכֶסֶף וְזָהָב יִרְבֶּה לָּךְ וְרָם לְבָבֶךָ וְשָׁכַחְתָּ אֶת ה׳״." "<b>Immediately a man wrestled with him.</b> Samael confronted him to bring him to utter forgetfulness of Eloah. And the word וַיֵּאָבֵק is like וַיְחַבֵּק ('and he embraced'), for the letters אח״הע are interchangeable, and it is written וַיֵּאָבֵק with an אָלֶ״ף in order to derive from it the meaning of אָבָק ('dust') — for just as he wished to blind the eye of his intellect like this dust that rises and blinds a person's eyes, so did it enter his mind to blind the eye of his intellect in order to bring him to denial of Eloah from above. And this is what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Chullin 91a), that the dust rose up to the Throne of Glory — for this is the blinding of the eyes of the righteous, touching the very essence of faith in the Creator, blessed be He, and the existence of the Throne of His Glory, blessed be He." "And what they said — that it touched the Throne of Glory — they learned from what is written, ״עַד עֲלוֹת הַשָּׁחַר״ ('until the breaking of dawn'), which implies 'until but not including,' for the breaking of dawn held him back, so that he could not bring him to denial of Eloah, because through the breaking of dawn he recognized in truth that the world has a Creator and Guide — as the author of the Akeidah explained in the portion of Bereshit on the verse ״וְיָדַעְתָּ הַיּוֹם וַהֲשֵׁבֹתָ אֶל לְבָבֶךָ כִּי ה׳ הוּא הָאֱלֹהִים״ (Deuteronomy 4:39): that through knowledge of the daily course of the sphere, that is, the sun, the contemplator can reflect and understand that HaShem is Elohim. And so too Abraham did not recognize his Creator except through the courses of the sun and the beginning of its ascent at the breaking of dawn, as it is written (Isaiah 41:2) ״מִי הֵעִיר מִמִּזְרָח צֶדֶק״, and this verse is expounded regarding Abraham. And this is the good pearl that hung on the neck of Abraham — that he proclaimed to all the existence of the El, blessed be He, and at his death he hung this knowledge upon the sphere of the sun. And the Rav explained that on this account they said (Yoma 20b): Were it not for the sphere of the sun, which proclaims the existence of the El, blessed be He, the voice of the tumult of Rome, which denies His existence, blessed be He, would be heard. Therefore it says ״עַד עֲלוֹת הַשָּׁחַר״ — and not including — for through knowledge of the movement of the sun, Jacob knew that HaShem is Elohim. And for this reason the judgment of the wicked is in the mornings, and a proof from Sodom, as it is written (Psalms 101:8) ״לַבְּקָרִים אַצְמִית כָּל רִשְׁעֵי אָרֶץ״ — because they did not take a lesson from the breaking of dawn and did not know HaShem." "And when Samael saw that he could not blind the eye of his intellect completely, he then strove with another stratagem to blind at least part of the eye of his intellect, so that he would not be able to see into the secrets of the Torah and the hidden matters stored in the thigh — as it is written (Song of Songs 7:2) ״חַמּוּקֵי יְרֵכַיִךְ״, and as they said: just as the thigh is hidden, so too words of Torah are hidden (Sukkah 49b). And this is what is written: ״וַיִּגַּע בְּכַף יְרֵכוֹ״. And I do not wish to remove this verse from its plain sense, for he certainly touched his thigh literally, but I am giving a reason for the matter of why he touched specifically the hollow of his thigh — namely, that he hinted to him that through having become a lover of money, he would without doubt be blinded of the eye of his intellect. And even if Jacob's merit held that he did not come to outright denial of Eloah at all, nevertheless the matter would touch the hollow of his thigh, for through this he would not be able to see into the secrets of Torah and wisdom. ״וַתֵּקַע כַּף יֶרֶךְ יַעֲקֹב״ — to instruct him that these two acquisitions, Torah and money, are rivals to one another. ״וַתֵּקַע״ — that is, the one is pushed aside before the other, as it is written (Psalms 119:71) ״טוֹב לִי כִי עֻנֵּיתִי לְמַעַן אֶלְמַד חֻקֶּיךָ״ ('It is good for me that I was afflicted, in order that I might learn Your statutes')."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {1 "The phrase מחבלים רמים ונשאים is rendered hyper-literally as 'high and exalted cables'; the text appears to be mid-metaphor, comparing the small jars to objects suspended by lofty ropes, and the passage is textually dense.", 3 "The Hebrew phrase מרגלית טובה ('good pearl') is a known Talmudic idiom (Shabbat 152a) for a cherished teaching; it may also allude to a physical amulet, but the translation preserves the idiom literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:32:15.585396912Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}